00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:03.350
Well, Bayesian inference is a big deal.

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:06.810
Lots of researchers have thought a lot about
it, and how it can be used to advantage in

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:07.810
data analysis.

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:12.830
There are a few books on the topic, and I
encourage you to take a closer look.

00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:17.150
My goal today is to be sort of a cheerleader
for Bayesian methods, pointing out some of

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:21.450
their basic features, and also some opportunities
in this new world of data science.

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:25.800
But, in addition to my job, there are some
things for you to do, too.

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:29.110
You can join the Society for Bayesian Analysis.

00:02:29.110 --> 00:02:33.280
You can check out the American Statistical
Association section.

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:37.190
You can check out online the journal, Bayesian
Analysis.

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:43.360
You could, in fact, visit Duke University,
where is one of the strong centers for Bayesian

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:46.250
inference and research in these techniques.

00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:48.410
You could check out some code.

00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:52.700
Andrew Gelman's originated this terrific code
project called Stan.

00:02:52.700 --> 00:03:01.910
It delivers some really useful software techniques
for deploying Bayesian analysis.

00:03:01.910 --> 00:03:03.460
So that's some of the things you can do.

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:08.650
I'm going to just give you a bit of an overview
of what Bayesian analysis is-- some of the

00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:12.010
key features about it, and how it goes in
some examples.

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:15.790
So it's a general strategy for data analysis,
and it's based on probability.

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:19.410
And the key thing about it is it couples data
with some kind of model

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:21.100
specification.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:23.300
And that model specification has two features.

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:28.570
It's got a probability structure for the data
themselves, given unknown things.

00:03:28.570 --> 00:03:33.350
This reflects kind of the central dogma of
statistics-- that all data sets are the realization

00:03:33.350 --> 00:03:35.130
of some random process.

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:38.150
And encoding that in probabilities is kind
of central.

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:43.910
But then the second part of it has to do with
all the unknown parameters, say.

00:03:43.910 --> 00:03:46.040
And there's some probability structure on
that, too.

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:48.200
That's a special part of Bayesian analysis.

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:53.069
It essentially expands the domain of probability
to be a general measure of uncertainty.

00:03:53.069 --> 00:03:59.210
And then inference about what we don't know,
given what we know, is perceived using the

00:03:59.210 --> 00:04:00.540
rules probability.

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:04.640
OK, well, I'm talking about a few relevant
examples.

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:10.200
But I wanted to start with a kind of irrelevant
example-- but it's still pedagogically interesting--

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:11.780
about thumb tacks.

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:13.320
So here's a little video.

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:15.400
I'm doing an experiment to collect some data.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:17.310
I'm tossing a thumb tack.

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:21.690
And notice that I tossed that tack, and it
landed with its point facing down.

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:27.160
I replicated that experiment again, and I
tossed the tack, and it happened to land with

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:30.139
its point facing up.

00:04:30.139 --> 00:04:34.949
And this was the beginning of a longer experiment
that I did, with 50 trials, the outcomes of

00:04:34.949 --> 00:04:37.249
which-- the full dataset-- is shown here.

00:04:37.249 --> 00:04:44.580
I got, in my 50 trials, it turned out, 17
times that the tack landed with its point

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:46.789
facing down.

00:04:46.789 --> 00:04:52.310
Now we know, from the law of large numbers,
the relative frequency at which the tack lands

00:04:52.310 --> 00:04:56.340
with its tip point facing down-- that D over
n.

00:04:56.340 --> 00:05:01.419
Relative frequency is-- well, it's a random
variable, for one thing-- but also, it stabilizes

00:05:01.419 --> 00:05:02.419
in the long run.

00:05:02.419 --> 00:05:07.080
If I were to have continued the experiment,
tossing tacks indefinitely, it would converge

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:08.770
to something we'll call theta.

00:05:08.770 --> 00:05:12.880
The long run relative frequency of times that
the tack lands with it's head pointing down.

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:16.729
And that's a parameter of the system-- a property
of the tack in the experimental system.

00:05:16.729 --> 00:05:23.270
And it's an unknown underlying parameter,
underneath the dataset.

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:28.039
So we could, of course, estimate that unknown
theta with 17 out of 50-- 34%.

00:05:28.039 --> 00:05:34.979
But at issue, really-- in our focus are asking
more general inference questions, such as,

00:05:34.979 --> 00:05:43.870
what are plausible values of that parameter,
having observed 17 tack points down in 50

00:05:43.870 --> 00:05:44.870
tests?

00:05:44.870 --> 00:05:49.349
And rather than go through the math of it,
I just wanted to answer this question a Bayesian

00:05:49.349 --> 00:05:52.110
would answer, but through a simulation.

00:05:52.110 --> 00:05:57.740
So in this first part of the simulation, suppose
we happen to know this long-run relative frequency,

00:05:57.740 --> 00:05:58.740
theta.

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:04.729
If we did, we could certainly run the experiment
in the computer and replicate it.

00:06:04.729 --> 00:06:12.979
So this little panel shows a sort of set up
to understand replications of this little

00:06:12.979 --> 00:06:14.979
tack tossing experiment.

00:06:14.979 --> 00:06:20.669
I've got possible values of theta on the horizontal,
and I've got possible values of data on the

00:06:20.669 --> 00:06:21.669
vertical.

00:06:21.669 --> 00:06:25.979
And the observed value of data is steep--
17 times that the tack faced pointing down

00:06:25.979 --> 00:06:27.900
out of 50 trials.

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:31.059
Now look at the little code chunk up in the
upper right, here.

00:06:31.059 --> 00:06:32.779
This is my in silico experiment.

00:06:32.779 --> 00:06:37.439
I could fix theta, for instance, if I knew
it, at, say, 60%.

00:06:37.439 --> 00:06:42.659
And set up this little code, which just is
a way of using pseudorandom numbers in the

00:06:42.659 --> 00:06:46.819
computer to simulate the tack tossing without
actually tossing the tack 50 times.

00:06:46.819 --> 00:06:50.900
But this a replicate of that, if
I knew that long run relative frequency.

00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:54.259
And I happen, when I do that, to get about
26.

00:06:54.259 --> 00:06:56.879
Now since it's a simple bit of code, I can
repeat it.

00:06:56.879 --> 00:06:59.410
Obviously I can repeat it.

00:06:59.410 --> 00:07:01.229
And look-- I did it about 1,000 times.

00:07:01.229 --> 00:07:04.300
And this represents the so-called sampling
distribution.

00:07:04.300 --> 00:07:10.369
You'll all recognize the binomial sampling
distribution of the relative values of the

00:07:10.369 --> 00:07:18.279
number of point-down tacks out of 50, centered
around 60% of 50-- around 30-- but with some

00:07:18.279 --> 00:07:19.789
fluctuation.

00:07:19.789 --> 00:07:27.309
And notice that the observed value of 17 doesn't
look very plausible for that dataset.

00:07:27.309 --> 00:07:32.069
And that's basically a problem with this little
simulation-- I've had to pretend I know the

00:07:32.069 --> 00:07:35.889
value of this unknown parameter, but in fact,
I don't.

00:07:35.889 --> 00:07:39.089
Now if I do that simulation, I could just
change it a little bit.

00:07:39.089 --> 00:07:46.099
If you look up here, I just imagined what
data I might have gotten.

00:07:46.099 --> 00:07:52.919
Let me also vary the value of the parameter
theta, by just grabbing a uniformly distributed

00:07:52.919 --> 00:07:54.449
theta every time.

00:07:54.449 --> 00:08:01.729
And on that uniformly distributed theta, then
go ahead and simulate my outcome of my experiment.

00:08:01.729 --> 00:08:06.939
This is what you'd get, if you do it about
1,000 times.

00:08:06.939 --> 00:08:12.419
Now what's round and gray there, is-- these
are hypothetical repetitions of the experiments

00:08:12.419 --> 00:08:17.599
that I've set up, in this two-stage experiment--
where the simulated data actually is exactly

00:08:17.599 --> 00:08:21.779
the same as the data that I happen to see
in my real experiment.

00:08:21.779 --> 00:08:27.749
So surely, those values or those parameters,
that gave rise to those simulated data, must

00:08:27.749 --> 00:08:28.819
be plausible.

00:08:28.819 --> 00:08:33.330
Because in light of the data that I saw, they
could easily have given rise to the same data,

00:08:33.330 --> 00:08:34.789
as we saw on the simulation.

00:08:34.789 --> 00:08:37.899
So that is the essence of what Bayesian analysis
is.

00:08:37.899 --> 00:08:40.450
It's looking at the distribution of those
theta values.

00:08:40.450 --> 00:08:46.391
Now we've conditioned, effectively, on that
17, and we can project down onto the theta

00:08:46.391 --> 00:08:48.740
axis right now.

00:08:48.740 --> 00:08:51.720
And so I've just taken this and boosted the
simulation size up a bit.

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:58.010
But this is the distribution of thetas that
came out of this experiment, where I first

00:08:58.010 --> 00:09:01.020
simulated theta, and then I get simulated
data, given theta.

00:09:01.020 --> 00:09:04.500
But then I keep track of the theta that matches
my observed data.

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:06.840
These are certainly plausible values.

00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:10.900
Well indeed, mathematically, we can work out
the formula for that curve.

00:09:10.900 --> 00:09:13.150
It's known as the posterior distribution.

00:09:13.150 --> 00:09:17.461
Functional form is known, and it expresses
a kind of uncertainty in the value of data

00:09:17.461 --> 00:09:21.890
that would have generated the data we saw,
but that uncertainty expressed after the data

00:09:21.890 --> 00:09:24.330
have been seen.

00:09:24.330 --> 00:09:28.130
That's to contrast it with the uncertainty
that we expressed in this little simulation

00:09:28.130 --> 00:09:29.620
before the data were seen.

00:09:29.620 --> 00:09:36.580
Remember that runif chunk of code that said
what the theta values are before, what theta

00:09:36.580 --> 00:09:41.160
values I'm considering as relevant, in advance
and having seen the data.

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:47.570
And this connection, the famous formula that
the posterior is proportional to the prior

00:09:47.570 --> 00:09:51.590
times the likelihood-- this likelihood now
coming from the binomial model, in our case--

00:09:51.590 --> 00:09:55.080
goes way back to Thomas Bayes himself.

00:09:55.080 --> 00:10:01.430
Now looking at that functional form, this
thing, this beast, is the object that is critical

00:10:01.430 --> 00:10:02.430
for inference.

00:10:02.430 --> 00:10:04.710
So we could take-we don't know that theta.

00:10:04.710 --> 00:10:05.710
We have a summary of our uncertainty about
it.

00:10:05.710 --> 00:10:06.710
If we take the center value, the mean of
we don't know that theta.

00:10:06.710 --> 00:10:07.710
We have a summary of our uncertainty about
it.

00:10:07.710 --> 00:10:13.340
If we take the center value, the mean of that
distribution-- its expected value-- that could

00:10:13.340 --> 00:10:15.390
serve as a point estimate.

00:10:15.390 --> 00:10:20.200
And that happens, in this case-- that the
balance point of the distribution is a little

00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:25.450
different than what you would think of as
the natural estimator, 17 or 50.

00:10:25.450 --> 00:10:27.320
It's a little bit modified.

00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:34.460
It's, as we say, shrunken towards the prior
mean, which is a half compared to the NLA.

00:10:34.460 --> 00:10:39.220
We could also get an interval that contains
a certain amount of posterior probably.

00:10:39.220 --> 00:10:43.210
So that's how it would go in this little toy
example.

00:10:43.210 --> 00:10:48.890
Now let's take a second, less-toy example--
intestinal tumors.

00:10:48.890 --> 00:10:53.710
Now here, the data aren't outcomes of a tack
throwing experiment, but they're outcomes

00:10:53.710 --> 00:11:03.390
of looking inside the DNA of polyps that were
found during colonoscopy of men in a study.

00:11:03.390 --> 00:11:06.480
Detectable pathogenic mutations from 36 colon
polyps.

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:08.500
Here's the data, here.

00:11:08.500 --> 00:11:12.720
The horizontal corresponds to the 36 different
subjects in the study.

00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:19.070
The vertical, or the rows, correspond to different
pathogenic mutations that were measured, assessed,

00:11:19.070 --> 00:11:23.980
by sequencing different genes that are known
to be critically involved in the development

00:11:23.980 --> 00:11:26.180
of colon cancer.

00:11:26.180 --> 00:11:27.180
That's the data.

00:11:27.180 --> 00:11:30.830
These are detectable, so they have to have
appeared at a sufficiently high frequency

00:11:30.830 --> 00:11:32.180
in the sequencing of the tumor.

00:11:32.180 --> 00:11:40.560
This comes from a recent publication by Chelsie
Sievers and Rich Halberg's group here in Madison.

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:48.970
OK, before I tell a bit about the model, I
have to say something about colon cancer progression.

00:11:48.970 --> 00:11:54.350
This little cartoon on the left shows just
a tiny segment of the wall of the intestinal

00:11:54.350 --> 00:11:55.940
epithelium.

00:11:55.940 --> 00:11:59.870
And the green piece here has been converted.

00:11:59.870 --> 00:12:01.810
It's initiated.

00:12:01.810 --> 00:12:05.530
It's non-normal, and it's starting to become
cancerous.

00:12:05.530 --> 00:12:07.730
And the polyps are essentially at this stage.

00:12:07.730 --> 00:12:13.640
The data is coming at this stage-- early abnormal
growth outside of this structure here.

00:12:13.640 --> 00:12:16.280
And eventually, if things go wrong, you will
get a cancer.

00:12:16.280 --> 00:12:18.792
But the data that we're talking about are
data early [INAUDIBLE] stage.

00:12:18.792 --> 00:12:26.380
I also want to point out a basic structural
unit of the intestinal wall that's expressed

00:12:26.380 --> 00:12:29.050
here in cross-section in this cartoon.

00:12:29.050 --> 00:12:35.310
The intestinal wall is made up of millions,
I guess, of these barrel-shaped glands called

00:12:35.310 --> 00:12:36.310
crypts.

00:12:36.310 --> 00:12:40.410
There are actually thousands of cells, not
just a handful of cells here.

00:12:40.410 --> 00:12:44.990
And they're clonal, so essentially they all
come from stem cells at the bottom of the

00:12:44.990 --> 00:12:47.200
space.

00:12:47.200 --> 00:12:48.830
They're from a single lineage.

00:12:48.830 --> 00:12:52.420
That's going to be important in something
I'll say later.

00:12:52.420 --> 00:12:54.340
They are from a single lineage.

00:12:54.340 --> 00:12:58.170
And they are the basic structural unit of
the intestine, like I said.

00:12:58.170 --> 00:13:03.600
And also tumors, when they start, initially
adenomas, they emerge out of these crypts,

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:04.710
and they start to grow.

00:13:04.710 --> 00:13:08.440
There's a whole bunch of interesting things
about crypt biology I won't say, but the crypt

00:13:08.440 --> 00:13:12.310
itself is going to be an important thing for
my story.

00:13:12.310 --> 00:13:18.820
OK, so previously we tossed tacks in silico,
but now instead of tossing tacks in silico,

00:13:18.820 --> 00:13:23.020
I'm going to do a simulation of tumor growth.

00:13:23.020 --> 00:13:26.430
It's a discrete-step simulation.

00:13:26.430 --> 00:13:30.670
It's not cells that are shown here, but actually
crypts that proceed.

00:13:30.670 --> 00:13:33.910
This evolved by a certain stochastic model,
which you can read about in the papers.

00:13:33.910 --> 00:13:35.570
Actually, crypts may fission.

00:13:35.570 --> 00:13:38.910
They turn into multiple crypts, or they potentially
die.

00:13:38.910 --> 00:13:43.680
And also, the crypts, these clonal bodies
may incur certain mutations as they go on.

00:13:43.680 --> 00:13:49.090
The colors that you may be able to see here--
there's different colors-- correspond to different

00:13:49.090 --> 00:13:51.560
mutations that have occurred and then grown
out.

00:13:51.560 --> 00:13:53.720
So this is an interesting thing.

00:13:53.720 --> 00:13:59.100
So it's not tossing a tack, but it's growing
a tumor.

00:13:59.100 --> 00:14:02.200
Now, what did Sievers do?

00:14:02.200 --> 00:14:05.560
They would simulate these tumors, and then
they'd mimic the sampling process.

00:14:05.560 --> 00:14:12.000
They did an in silico section of the tumor,
looked at the crypts inside.

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:15.970
Basically, we're trying to match what they
observed here with our observed data on the

00:14:15.970 --> 00:14:17.260
36.

00:14:17.260 --> 00:14:19.630
In the Sievers paper, they do something very
light.

00:14:19.630 --> 00:14:24.370
Instead of conditioning on all 36, they just
ask a very, very simple question.

00:14:24.370 --> 00:14:27.030
It's a little bit different than finishing
all the data.

00:14:27.030 --> 00:14:31.450
They just asked, at the level of a mutation
that you would see in here, does the frequency

00:14:31.450 --> 00:14:38.260
at which that mutation occur, does it exist
at a sufficiently high frequency to be detected

00:14:38.260 --> 00:14:40.830
by the next-generation sequencing technology
that's used?

00:14:40.830 --> 00:14:45.550
And there's a lower threshold, that the mutant
frequency had to be bigger than 10% in order

00:14:45.550 --> 00:14:47.260
for the sequencing to observe.

00:14:47.260 --> 00:14:48.680
Is it a detectable mutation?

00:14:48.680 --> 00:14:55.580
Now, ideally, in fact, we would simulate not
just one tumor up to check for its mutation,

00:14:55.580 --> 00:14:59.290
but we would simulate a sample of these polyps
or tumors.

00:14:59.290 --> 00:15:07.040
And you'd see if the mutation profile in slices
of these things matched our observed sample.

00:15:07.040 --> 00:15:08.040
We don't do that.

00:15:08.040 --> 00:15:09.040
Sievers didn't do that.

00:15:09.040 --> 00:15:13.840
But I'd just point interested observers to
this paper, just came out earlier this week,

00:15:13.840 --> 00:15:18.910
by Marjoram's group that does essentially
that kind of a calculus.

00:15:18.910 --> 00:15:24.000
In any case, in the simple calculation that
we're talking about, where we simulate up--

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:29.060
instead of tossing a tack, we're simulating
a tumor that's growing in the intestine through

00:15:29.060 --> 00:15:35.040
a stochastic model of how tumors might grow,
and we look at a mutation that's shown up

00:15:35.040 --> 00:15:37.300
at the time that we observe the tumor.

00:15:37.300 --> 00:15:44.300
The quantity of interest in Sievers' paper
was the size of the tumor in crypts when the

00:15:44.300 --> 00:15:47.760
mutation that we're looking at happened to
have emerged.

00:15:47.760 --> 00:15:54.050
So when we see it at observation time, we
get to see a mutation at some frequency.

00:15:54.050 --> 00:15:58.640
But that mutation must have emerged in the
tumor growth sometime before we got to observe

00:15:58.640 --> 00:15:59.640
it.

00:15:59.640 --> 00:16:01.750
That time isn't known to the observer.

00:16:01.750 --> 00:16:04.160
That's the parameter theta sub mu.

00:16:04.160 --> 00:16:09.820
And the simulation's rigged up so that these
mutations can emerge at any time along the--

00:16:09.820 --> 00:16:17.150
they can emerge at any time, in the sense
of any size that the tumor is as it's growing.

00:16:17.150 --> 00:16:22.220
But if we condition on the data, so to speak,
that we condition on the fact that the mutation

00:16:22.220 --> 00:16:27.380
actually exists at sufficiently high frequency
to be detectable when we measure it, then

00:16:27.380 --> 00:16:33.100
we get a posterior distribution of the size
of the tumor when that mutation arose.

00:16:33.100 --> 00:16:36.740
And looking at these kind of distributions
tells us that these detectable

00:16:36.740 --> 00:16:39.990
mutations must have arisen when the tumor
was actually quite small.

00:16:39.990 --> 00:16:43.500
And you can get specific inferences about
that.

00:16:43.500 --> 00:16:48.440
So that's an interesting variation of the
simulation story, where instead of simulating

00:16:48.440 --> 00:16:50.320
tacks, we're simulating tumors.

00:16:50.320 --> 00:16:57.150
But the same story-- you simulate unknown
things, data-- simulated data until that data

00:16:57.150 --> 00:17:01.279
is equal or approximately equal to the observed
data that you have.

00:17:01.279 --> 00:17:05.000
And you keep track of the quantity theta that
corresponds to it.

00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:11.180
This simulation view of the Bayesian inference
was one of a number of terrific insights made

00:17:11.180 --> 00:17:17.299
by Don Rubin in his paper that I recommend
highly to everybody on the frequency calculations

00:17:17.299 --> 00:17:20.640
that are relevant for Bayesian analysis.

00:17:20.640 --> 00:17:24.980
That's also the basis of what people are gonna
call approximate Bayesian computation.

00:17:24.980 --> 00:17:29.770
That's especially useful in situations where
the likelihood functions are intractable,

00:17:29.770 --> 00:17:35.750
such as in population genetics or other forms
of ancestral influence.

00:17:35.750 --> 00:17:40.720
So that's just a little, I suppose, to whet
your appetite about Bayes from a simulation

00:17:40.720 --> 00:17:41.820
perspective.

00:17:41.820 --> 00:17:50.050
I want to talk about three main features of
Bayes going forward now with the rest of my

00:17:50.050 --> 00:17:51.050
time.

00:17:51.050 --> 00:17:54.250
So I want to talk about three basic things--
handling uncertainty, combining different

00:17:54.250 --> 00:17:55.720
data sources, and borrowing strength.

00:17:55.720 --> 00:18:01.550
And I'll use three different case study examples
to illustrate these features of Bayesian inference.

00:18:01.550 --> 00:18:03.000
So let's go forward.

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:11.690
Handling uncertainty-- so this is one of the
sort of basic features of Bayes, that we understand

00:18:11.690 --> 00:18:14.850
that data doesn't tell us all the information.

00:18:14.850 --> 00:18:18.900
Even if it's big data, it doesn't have-- for
things we're interested about, it may not

00:18:18.900 --> 00:18:20.180
contain all the information.

00:18:20.180 --> 00:18:24.400
So we have to be able to express our uncertainty
about things that we don't quite know.

00:18:24.400 --> 00:18:25.740
And Bayes is rigged up for that.

00:18:25.740 --> 00:18:29.550
So let's say that in addition to a parameter
theta that we might be interested in, there's

00:18:29.550 --> 00:18:32.740
some other parameter, psi.

00:18:32.740 --> 00:18:37.790
And Bayes' procedure somehow or other through
a model specification gives rise to a joint

00:18:37.790 --> 00:18:42.430
posterior distribution of all the unknowns
given the data that we have.

00:18:42.430 --> 00:25:34.220
Well, if we're really just interested in theta,
somehow, then-

00:25:34.220 --> 00:30:08.100
Michael, we're having problems with the audio.

00:30:08.100 --> 00:30:13.900
Could you please check your microphone?

00:30:13.900 --> 00:30:16.180
Hello?

00:30:16.180 --> 00:30:18.460
Hello?

00:30:18.460 --> 00:30:25.300
Ah, you're back.

00:30:25.300 --> 00:30:31.540
I think that sounds better.

00:30:31.540 --> 00:30:34.070
OK, keep going.

00:30:34.070 --> 00:30:35.850
Well, should I back up some?

00:30:35.850 --> 00:30:41.419
I mean, I don't know how long I was-
We lost you for actually a few minutes, so

00:30:41.419 --> 00:30:46.260
maybe if you could just back up a little bit,
that would be good.

00:30:46.260 --> 00:30:47.260
OK.

00:30:47.260 --> 00:30:48.260
Sorry about that.

00:30:48.260 --> 00:30:49.260
I don't know quite what happened.

00:30:49.260 --> 00:30:50.260
Let's see if I can back up here.

00:30:50.260 --> 00:30:54.850
Give me a sign here.

00:30:54.850 --> 00:30:58.010
Do we know about this part?

00:30:58.010 --> 00:31:00.400
I think that's good.

00:31:00.400 --> 00:31:04.770
OK, so the handling uncertainty part, I'm
gonna try and talk about it in the context

00:31:04.770 --> 00:31:05.770
of an example.

00:31:05.770 --> 00:31:09.660
Would that be a good place to kick back in,
Alex?

00:31:09.660 --> 00:31:10.740
Yes.

00:31:10.740 --> 00:31:11.830
OK.

00:31:11.830 --> 00:31:15.090
OK, sorry folks.

00:31:15.090 --> 00:31:21.309
So this handling uncertainty, I'm going to
talk about in an example from RNA interference.

00:31:21.309 --> 00:31:26.590
This is a genome-wide technology for understanding
gene functions.

00:31:26.590 --> 00:31:32.470
And just briefly about that, you've seen the
slides but didn't hear me say it's a cell-based

00:31:32.470 --> 00:31:33.470
assay.

00:31:33.470 --> 00:31:37.740
The basic idea is you have a target gene that
you're targeting with a given siRNA.

00:31:37.740 --> 00:31:42.309
You add that siRNA-- small interfering RNA--
to the cells.

00:31:42.309 --> 00:31:46.320
And what it's supposed to do is knock down
the expression of that gene.

00:31:46.320 --> 00:31:50.900
And if that targeted gene is involved in some
phenotype that you're measuring, then that

00:31:50.900 --> 00:31:57.540
phenotype will change, and the cell will change
as I've indicated with the [INAUDIBLE].

00:31:57.540 --> 00:32:00.710
And in the story, we're talking about the
phenotype that has to do with the cell's ability

00:32:00.710 --> 00:32:02.260
to replicate virus.

00:32:02.260 --> 00:32:07.260
There were four large-scale studies that used
this technology genome-wide.

00:32:07.260 --> 00:32:11.310
And we had data on parts of these studies.

00:32:11.310 --> 00:32:13.650
All studies had an interesting similarity.

00:32:13.650 --> 00:32:18.530
They were two-stage studies with a detection
screen and a confirmation screen.

00:32:18.530 --> 00:32:24.679
The whole genome was-- every one of the genes,
different siRNAs were used to try to knock

00:32:24.679 --> 00:32:29.390
down things, and anything that passed a detection
screen went into a confirmation screen that

00:32:29.390 --> 00:32:35.670
was more intensive, but it only had to be
applied to the things that were detected initially.

00:32:35.670 --> 00:32:39.960
And remember, the detection has to do with
whether or not-the phenotype here is whether

00:32:39.960 --> 00:32:45.320
or not a cell, when it's infected with virus
and you knock down the gene, whether that

00:32:45.320 --> 00:32:49.900
changed the cell's ability to make virus.

00:32:49.900 --> 00:32:57.780
And one of the curious things here, as in
other examples, is that, as I was saying,

00:32:57.780 --> 00:33:04.410
each of the studies found about 200 genes
that they said when you knock them down, it

00:33:04.410 --> 00:33:07.470
affects the cell's ability to replicate virus.

00:33:07.470 --> 00:33:11.520
But only one gene was found in common to all
four studies.

00:33:11.520 --> 00:33:15.210
Few genes were found in three of the four
studies, some in two of the four, but the

00:33:15.210 --> 00:33:20.090
majority of genes that were identified and
reported as involved in flu were uniquely

00:33:20.090 --> 00:33:22.540
identified in one study only.

00:33:22.540 --> 00:33:24.000
Could be because of false positives.

00:33:24.000 --> 00:33:29.160
Measurement error is one, but off targets
is a big possibility, where you add the siRNA,

00:33:29.160 --> 00:33:33.380
and there's a change in the cell, as indicated
by the pink, but actually the gene that that

00:33:33.380 --> 00:33:36.750
siRNA has targeted isn't really involved in
flu at all.

00:33:36.750 --> 00:33:41.330
It's just that the siRNA accidentally, and
not by design, happens to knock down another

00:33:41.330 --> 00:33:43.270
gene that is involved in flu.

00:33:43.270 --> 00:33:46.809
And then you see the phenotype, even though
you shouldn't have really.

00:33:46.809 --> 00:33:51.460
It could also be false negatives, and there's
a bunch of possible false negative results.

00:33:51.460 --> 00:33:58.820
One where it's just-- the gene's really involved
in flu, but you're somehow not able to see

00:33:58.820 --> 00:34:00.600
it when you do the siRNA screening.

00:34:00.600 --> 00:34:07.000
For instance, you add the siRNA screen, you
knock down the gene that's really involved

00:34:07.000 --> 00:34:11.000
in flu, but somehow or other there's another
gene sitting there that's doing the job of

00:34:11.000 --> 00:34:15.500
the first gene in those particular cells,
and so even though the gene one has been knocked

00:34:15.500 --> 00:34:20.099
down and is really involved, you don't see
a phenotypic effect in those cells.

00:34:20.099 --> 00:34:24.040
There's various other forms for the negatives
and positives, but the big issue was whether

00:34:24.040 --> 00:34:32.790
or not-- which is the source of disagreement?

00:34:32.790 --> 00:34:39.310
If false positives were really a big deal,
you wouldn't be surprised to see a lack of

00:34:39.310 --> 00:34:43.629
agreement, because each study is just pulling
up a bunch of random genes, and so it wouldn't

00:34:43.629 --> 00:34:45.240
be surprising that you wouldn't have much
agreement.

00:34:45.240 --> 00:34:49.621
But at the same time, it could be that flu
is really a big complicated thing, and that

00:34:49.621 --> 00:34:53.599
each of the studies really is just seeing
a small part of it.

00:34:53.599 --> 00:34:57.320
And [INAUDIBLE] underlie those singletons
really are true.

00:34:57.320 --> 00:35:03.240
So as I was saying here, to bring it back
to the context of Bayesian analysis, we have

00:35:03.240 --> 00:35:07.120
a probability model for the data we were able
to compile, and the data that we're able to

00:35:07.120 --> 00:35:13.260
compile is a kind of meta-analysis of what
happens to each gene across each of the studies,

00:35:13.260 --> 00:35:15.480
whether or not it was detected and whether
or not it was confirmed.

00:35:15.480 --> 00:35:19.010
And we have counts, for instance, of how many
genes.

00:35:19.010 --> 00:35:21.790
There were four genes that were detected in
the first study and confirmed.

00:35:21.790 --> 00:35:24.430
They were detected in the second study and
not confirmed, and they weren't detected in

00:35:24.430 --> 00:35:26.530
the second two studies.

00:35:26.530 --> 00:35:31.050
Naturally, a multinomial likelihood.

00:35:31.050 --> 00:35:35.950
And that's the green, is the data that we
have, the detection and confirmation information.

00:35:35.950 --> 00:35:37.260
But we don't know a lot of things.

00:35:37.260 --> 00:35:39.420
We don't know whether the gene's really involved
in flu.

00:35:39.420 --> 00:35:43.030
We don't know if it's accessible or if there's
redundancies.

00:35:43.030 --> 00:35:48.539
In that case, we don't know off targets in
that study for those siRNA is used.

00:35:48.539 --> 00:35:51.980
And we don't know a bunch of things generally
about the system as a whole, about the rates

00:35:51.980 --> 00:35:53.490
of measurement error and efficiency.

00:35:53.490 --> 00:35:57.870
We have bits of information about that, but
all those things we don't know.

00:35:57.870 --> 00:36:02.540
We can put our heads together and try and
figure out a probability model that links

00:36:02.540 --> 00:36:06.010
together all those things we don't know with
what we know.

00:36:06.010 --> 00:36:10.190
You often see these [INAUDIBLE] basically
graphical representations of probability models

00:36:10.190 --> 00:36:14.190
to express dependencies, and that's what that
is here.

00:36:14.190 --> 00:36:18.300
So we use the tools of Bayesian analysis to
come up with a probability distribution of

00:36:18.300 --> 00:36:24.369
the unknowns, given from what we do see.

00:36:24.369 --> 00:36:27.880
So without going into any of the details of
how the calculations go ahead, we work out

00:36:27.880 --> 00:36:32.951
a joint posterior distribution, and then we
come down to the statement of marginal posterior

00:36:32.951 --> 00:36:37.180
distributions on quantities that we're actually
directly interested in-- these error rates.

00:36:37.180 --> 00:36:40.070
So for example, theta 1 is the quantity of
interest.

00:36:40.070 --> 00:36:42.700
It's the false discovery rate.

00:36:42.700 --> 00:36:44.859
It's something we don't know in advance, really.

00:36:44.859 --> 00:36:50.100
It's the chance that the gene would really
be involved in flu if, in fact, it was confirmed

00:36:50.100 --> 00:36:55.430
in the study.

00:36:55.430 --> 00:36:59.190
It's the chance that the gene is not involved
in flu, and that's [INAUDIBLE] given that

00:36:59.190 --> 00:37:00.190
it's confirmed.

00:37:00.190 --> 00:37:01.210
So if it's confirmed, what's the chances it's
not involved?

00:37:01.210 --> 00:37:02.960
That's a false discovery rate.

00:37:02.960 --> 00:37:05.140
This is a kind of a false positive rate.

00:37:05.140 --> 00:37:07.790
Well, we don't know that false positive rate.

00:37:07.790 --> 00:37:12.830
But in our calculation, we get a posterior
distribution for the false positive rate.

00:37:12.830 --> 00:37:13.830
That's way down here.

00:37:13.830 --> 00:37:18.030
And similarly, we get a distribution for the
false negative rate.

00:37:18.030 --> 00:37:22.680
And this and some other similar calculations,
basically they've accounted for all of the

00:37:22.680 --> 00:37:26.340
uncertainty, and all those many, many things
that we didn't know in the context of a specific

00:37:26.340 --> 00:37:29.220
probability specification.

00:37:29.220 --> 00:37:34.840
And it tells us pretty unequivocally that
false negatives are a bigger problem than

00:37:34.840 --> 00:37:36.040
false positives.

00:37:36.040 --> 00:37:39.540
And that lines up with some other analyses
of the same data.

00:37:39.540 --> 00:37:46.320
But that's an example of using marginal posteriors
to get particular quantities of interest and

00:37:46.320 --> 00:37:47.320
address those.

00:37:47.320 --> 00:37:56.460
I want to move on to another example-- combining
data.

00:37:56.460 --> 00:38:01.500
One of the big things about Bayes is that
you can combine multiple data sources.

00:38:01.500 --> 00:38:09.330
So I'm gonna talk about different data, x
and y coming together to make inference.

00:38:09.330 --> 00:38:16.710
This is a story about cancer, and it's based
on this amazing creature called an aggregation

00:38:16.710 --> 00:38:21.670
chimera that is formed from two early embryos
that are fused together and implanted into

00:38:21.670 --> 00:38:22.950
a foster mother.

00:38:22.950 --> 00:38:27.430
And then once in a while, if it all works
right you get some animal out that's really

00:38:27.430 --> 00:38:31.790
two separate animals more or less merged together--
four parents.

00:38:31.790 --> 00:38:37.400
Here's a chunk of the intestinal epithelium
of one of these animals that's also been genetically

00:38:37.400 --> 00:38:43.400
engineered to be susceptible to growing tumors
in their intestine.

00:38:43.400 --> 00:38:49.859
And it's blue and white color owing to the
two separate parental lineages.

00:38:49.859 --> 00:38:52.770
The blues come from one side, and the whites
come from another.

00:38:52.770 --> 00:38:56.970
Now, curiously, in the middle-- it's a little
hard to see, but in the middle is a tumor.

00:38:56.970 --> 00:39:00.830
And what's especially curious about it is
that it's two colors.

00:39:00.830 --> 00:39:02.320
It's blue and white.

00:39:02.320 --> 00:39:07.290
About 20 percent, in fact, of the tumors is
this animal are heterotypic.

00:39:07.290 --> 00:39:13.530
Now, the standard story about biology, cancer
growth, which of course we can't-- one of

00:39:13.530 --> 00:39:18.480
the cool things about cancer research is that--
statistics for cancer research is you're talking

00:39:18.480 --> 00:39:21.750
about events that happen before you're able
to observe them.

00:39:21.750 --> 00:39:27.359
But the standard story about tumors growing
out of crypts, crypts being clonal, doesn't

00:39:27.359 --> 00:39:31.360
work
here, because if it did, they would be either

00:39:31.360 --> 00:39:36.230
pure white, because the cells in one lineage
are either white or pure blue, but they wouldn't

00:39:36.230 --> 00:39:37.460
be mixed together.

00:39:37.460 --> 00:39:40.349
So this is a kind of a polyclonality story.

00:39:40.349 --> 00:39:43.500
Now, there's a bunch of interesting statistical
issues with the polyclonality.

00:39:43.500 --> 00:39:50.990
I just want to focus on one specific one,
about combining information sources.

00:39:50.990 --> 00:39:56.359
So one information source has to do with the
patchwork of the cell tissue of this lineage.

00:39:56.359 --> 00:39:58.619
Some of the cells come from one lineage.

00:39:58.619 --> 00:40:01.180
One of the originating embryo [INAUDIBLE]
comes from the other, so that patchwork, the

00:40:01.180 --> 00:40:04.580
beautiful patchwork that presents itself.

00:40:04.580 --> 00:40:10.100
Now, we'd like to know-- in order to have
an understanding of tumor initiation events,

00:40:10.100 --> 00:40:12.400
we'd like to know where the crypts are under
there.

00:40:12.400 --> 00:40:14.020
But we don't get to see those.

00:40:14.020 --> 00:40:18.090
Now, on the other hand, there's something
that-- well, just to back up, there's something

00:40:18.090 --> 00:40:23.152
about the imaging technique that is not able
to reveal the positions of the crypts in the

00:40:23.152 --> 00:40:24.470
blue and white images.

00:40:24.470 --> 00:40:30.089
Now, there is data on other animals that aren't
animals that are chimeric animals, but there

00:40:30.089 --> 00:40:33.230
are data on other animals-- or at least they're
not blue-white imaged animals-- that show

00:40:33.230 --> 00:40:36.300
what these crypts' organization look like.

00:40:36.300 --> 00:40:39.990
So we'd like to kind of merge-- fuse this
data with this data somehow.

00:40:39.990 --> 00:40:43.770
And that's what the analysis that I'll show
tries to do.

00:40:43.770 --> 00:40:51.119
The reason we want to do this is because we're
trying to understand how many pairs of crypts

00:40:51.119 --> 00:40:56.320
are really on edges between blue and white
that could potentially give rise to these

00:40:56.320 --> 00:40:58.000
clonal tumors.

00:40:58.000 --> 00:41:06.720
So the data one side, the X, is a pixel image,
a blue-white image, the unknown quantity theta,

00:41:06.720 --> 00:41:12.339
the positions that we record as centers of
positions of crypts.

00:41:12.339 --> 00:41:16.800
And it's easy with some simple image analysis
ideas to work out a likelihood.

00:41:16.800 --> 00:41:20.500
If you knew where the crypts were, what's
the probability of seeing a particular image?

00:41:20.500 --> 00:41:23.130
You know that the crypts are pure blue or
white.

00:41:23.130 --> 00:41:28.470
There would be some flip error at some small
rate which would flip a pixel.

00:41:28.470 --> 00:41:30.930
If it's supposed to be white, it might be
blue.

00:41:30.930 --> 00:41:34.830
And then if you're a pixel that's not over
a crypt, you might be coming up [INAUDIBLE]

00:41:34.830 --> 00:41:35.830
background rate.

00:41:35.830 --> 00:41:37.760
So that's one part.

00:41:37.760 --> 00:41:43.200
Now, we also have a second data source, I
was saying, about the positions of crypts,

00:41:43.200 --> 00:41:47.060
not in the data that we're interested in,
the blue-white image, but in another source.

00:41:47.060 --> 00:41:50.339
We can triangulate that image and look at
those points.

00:41:50.339 --> 00:41:52.480
They're not scattered uniformly randomly around.

00:41:52.480 --> 00:41:57.890
In fact, they have quite a bit of spatial
structure that we can express with a kind

00:41:57.890 --> 00:42:04.410
of spatial model on positions, where we use
the actual entry point distances to get an

00:42:04.410 --> 00:42:09.600
estimate of this potential function for the
distribution of spatial positions.

00:42:09.600 --> 00:42:11.110
So we've [INAUDIBLE] put that together.

00:42:11.110 --> 00:42:16.650
We use the external data Y to tell us about
what kind of patterns we might expect to see

00:42:16.650 --> 00:42:22.500
in the crypt orientation theta, and we want
to use that to get-- we take the blue-white

00:42:22.500 --> 00:42:28.450
image together to try and figure out what
the crypt position might have been, in fact.

00:42:28.450 --> 00:42:34.440
This is one sample from the posterior distribution
of the crypt position that I blow up here,

00:42:34.440 --> 00:42:38.140
and I've colored the
crypts either blue or white, depending on

00:42:38.140 --> 00:42:41.200
the majority color of the pixels above them.

00:42:41.200 --> 00:42:43.770
And the key thing had to do with these red
edges.

00:42:43.770 --> 00:42:48.060
These are neighboring crypts that are of opposite
color.

00:42:48.060 --> 00:42:52.350
So if a tumor might emerge from crypts nearby
like that, there would be a certain rate at

00:42:52.350 --> 00:42:54.700
which that happens that we can estimate.

00:42:54.700 --> 00:43:00.160
And then based on this, we could get some
idea about what the interaction distance might

00:43:00.160 --> 00:43:06.160
be in between crypts that are at the origin
of polyclonal tumors.

00:43:06.160 --> 00:43:09.210
OK, that's a quick run-through of combining
information.

00:43:09.210 --> 00:43:16.060
And in the last part, I want in the last few
minutes to talk about borrowing information--

00:43:16.060 --> 00:43:18.150
borrowing strength, or information sharing.

00:43:18.150 --> 00:43:21.750
This is a big deal in genomics.

00:43:21.750 --> 00:43:26.480
You've got possibly many different but related
inference problems.

00:43:26.480 --> 00:43:30.390
There are m of them, and I've got thetas are
the unknown parameters in each one.

00:43:30.390 --> 00:43:32.250
And each one gives rise to a data set.

00:43:32.250 --> 00:43:35.060
They're separate but related, in some sense.

00:43:35.060 --> 00:43:37.140
Now, there's two cases.

00:43:37.140 --> 00:43:41.760
The problems may be linked by some other parameter,
psi.

00:43:41.760 --> 00:43:43.359
We don't know any other thetas or the psis.

00:43:43.359 --> 00:43:44.910
We get the data.

00:43:44.910 --> 00:43:50.300
Now, it's a classic result, going a long time
ago, that if you're interested in inference

00:43:50.300 --> 00:43:53.859
about the psi, that unknown linking parameter,
you could screw up.

00:43:53.859 --> 00:43:58.160
Even if you get lots and lots of data, your
standard estimates-- simple maximum likelihood

00:43:58.160 --> 00:44:01.430
estimate-- won't be consistent for that underlying
parameter.

00:44:01.430 --> 00:44:06.020
Goes back to Newman and Scott, 1948.

00:44:06.020 --> 00:44:11.510
Just a few years later, though, Kiefer and
Wolfowitz showed you could do a trick.

00:44:11.510 --> 00:44:15.070
You've got a lot of those theta parameters,
and if instead of just treating them as unknown

00:44:15.070 --> 00:44:20.690
quantities, you treated them as unknown quantities
that came from a distribution, and you accounted

00:44:20.690 --> 00:44:23.550
for the fact that there was such a distribution.

00:44:23.550 --> 00:44:31.430
Even if you didn't know it, you could get
an estimate that now is still an MLA, a maximum

00:44:31.430 --> 00:44:35.640
likelihood estimate, but it's acknowledging
that the thetas are coming from a distribution

00:44:35.640 --> 00:44:36.930
of essentially random effects.

00:44:36.930 --> 00:44:40.560
Then you could get consistent estimates of
the unknown.

00:44:40.560 --> 00:44:43.290
So there was something just about having lots
of parameters.

00:44:43.290 --> 00:44:45.780
When you had them, you ought to treat them
as if they're from a population.

00:44:45.780 --> 00:44:48.020
That turned out to be important.

00:44:48.020 --> 00:44:53.020
And here's another related thing that happened
long ago, too.

00:44:53.020 --> 00:44:54.020
Here's a parameter.

00:44:54.020 --> 00:44:55.540
I'll tell the story in three dimensions.

00:44:55.540 --> 00:44:58.660
There's three unknown related but separate
problems.

00:44:58.660 --> 00:45:01.040
Three unknown quantities-- theta 1, theta
2.

00:45:01.040 --> 00:45:04.700
And I'm going to get three data points-- D1,
D2, D3.

00:45:04.700 --> 00:45:10.200
And the sampling model is that the data are
independent, centered on theta.

00:45:10.200 --> 00:45:13.350
So D1 is a data measurement that's estimating
theta 1.

00:45:13.350 --> 00:45:14.770
D2 is estimating theta 2.

00:45:14.770 --> 00:45:16.190
D3 is estimating theta 3.

00:45:16.190 --> 00:45:20.110
But they're independent things, so D1 has
no effect on D2, and so on.

00:45:20.110 --> 00:45:28.359
The little code chunk on the right is actually
trying to simulate that-- simulates this for

00:45:28.359 --> 00:45:29.359
various thetas.

00:45:29.359 --> 00:45:34.320
In fact, in this little code, I want to fix
theta 1, fix theta 2, and I want to run on

00:45:34.320 --> 00:45:37.000
a grid of theta 3 to show a picture.

00:45:37.000 --> 00:45:39.680
I simulate up some data.

00:45:39.680 --> 00:45:44.940
Now, the question is, if I use the data--
I want to use the data somehow to estimate

00:45:44.940 --> 00:45:47.330
the unknown quantities, the thetas.

00:45:47.330 --> 00:45:48.619
There's no psi in this story.

00:45:48.619 --> 00:45:51.450
There's just unknown thetas.

00:45:51.450 --> 00:45:55.359
I could use a standard estimate, which is
to use the data themselves as estimates of

00:45:55.359 --> 00:45:56.520
their expected values.

00:45:56.520 --> 00:46:02.330
That's the standard estimate, which I calculate
in this code, and I look at error of that

00:46:02.330 --> 00:46:06.150
estimate in a way I'll show in a second.

00:46:06.150 --> 00:46:08.990
And then there's another one, a Bayes-style
estimate.

00:46:08.990 --> 00:46:10.260
I'll call it just Bayes-style.

00:46:10.260 --> 00:46:12.290
It's the data, but I shrink it a little bit.

00:46:12.290 --> 00:46:16.550
Just like back in the tack problem, where
there was shrinkage, there's a little shrinkage

00:46:16.550 --> 00:46:20.460
here that is a factor that, in fact, depends
on the data.

00:46:20.460 --> 00:46:24.070
Now, this is the plot of the error.

00:46:24.070 --> 00:46:28.500
The separate but related problems had nothing
to do with each other.

00:46:28.500 --> 00:46:30.349
The noise had nothing to do with it.

00:46:30.349 --> 00:46:35.950
But I'm going to assess the error of an estimator
by combining the problems together, and look

00:46:35.950 --> 00:46:40.630
at the average squared difference between
the estimator and the thing it's estimating

00:46:40.630 --> 00:46:43.010
accumulated up over the three different problems.

00:46:43.010 --> 00:46:47.109
If I took the standard estimator, that mean
squared error is in the blue here, according

00:46:47.109 --> 00:46:48.660
to that simulation.

00:46:48.660 --> 00:46:53.490
I'm fixing theta 1, theta 2, and I'm just
letting theta 3 vary, just because it's hard

00:46:53.490 --> 00:46:55.880
to draw in three dimensions.

00:46:55.880 --> 00:46:59.920
If instead I was looking at the error not
of the standard estimator, but the error here

00:46:59.920 --> 00:47:04.280
of the Bayes estimator, I get Bayes-style
estimator, I'll call it.

00:47:04.280 --> 00:47:08.590
You get uniformly smaller-- uniformly smaller.

00:47:08.590 --> 00:47:14.359
So this is a fundamental finding from way
back, that if performance evaluation considers

00:47:14.359 --> 00:47:20.089
all the tasks that once, all those related
but separate problems, then the ideal procedure

00:47:20.089 --> 00:47:25.570
for the whole thing is not taken by assembling
the ideal procedures for each problem taken

00:47:25.570 --> 00:47:31.451
in isolation-- a fundamental fact that James
Stein figured out, and that in fact, you can

00:47:31.451 --> 00:47:37.390
actually do better in frequency terms by using
some kind of a Bayesian-style procedure.

00:47:37.390 --> 00:47:41.390
OK, how does this actually work?

00:47:41.390 --> 00:47:44.460
Well, go back to the Keifer-Wolfowitz story.

00:47:44.460 --> 00:47:47.990
You've got a bunch of related but separate
problems.

00:47:47.990 --> 00:47:53.770
I have unknown quantities, data, and I'm going
to now treat them as if they come from a distribution

00:47:53.770 --> 00:47:54.810
in order to try and get some benefit.

00:47:54.810 --> 00:47:59.990
But of course, in reality, I don't know--
I know the data, but I wouldn't know the distribution

00:47:59.990 --> 00:48:01.310
that generated it.

00:48:01.310 --> 00:48:06.730
Of course, I have data, so maybe I could somehow
or other estimate that distribution.

00:48:06.730 --> 00:48:11.170
Now, as I've estimated it, now I can think
like a Bayesian and say, well, I have that

00:48:11.170 --> 00:48:17.510
distribution called g, which is essentially
[INAUDIBLE] and formulate a prior distribution

00:48:17.510 --> 00:48:22.980
before I get data, essentially, associated
with all the parameters.

00:48:22.980 --> 00:48:27.740
So I can do a Bayesian-style calculation by
working out the posterior proportional to

00:48:27.740 --> 00:48:30.430
that prior times the likelihood piece.

00:48:30.430 --> 00:48:34.710
And here-- this is the borrowing information
part-- this actually-- I'm doing inference

00:48:34.710 --> 00:48:42.060
on a single unit, but this has acquired information
from all of the other units in this two-stage

00:48:42.060 --> 00:48:43.060
process.

00:48:43.060 --> 00:48:47.070
OK, so borrowing information is used a lot
in genomics.

00:48:47.070 --> 00:48:49.900
I'm just going to close out with an example
of RNA-seq, is

00:48:49.900 --> 00:48:52.680
used for measuring transcripts quickly.

00:48:52.680 --> 00:48:53.870
We get samples of cells.

00:48:53.870 --> 00:48:57.150
Messenger RNAs are extracted and converted
to cDNA.

00:48:57.150 --> 00:49:00.060
They're read by next-gen sequencing reads.

00:49:00.060 --> 00:49:01.680
Those reads are aligned with the genome.

00:49:01.680 --> 00:49:07.610
We get some measures-- rough, raw measurements
of how frequent those RNAs are in the sample

00:49:07.610 --> 00:49:08.610
of cells.

00:49:08.610 --> 00:49:11.650
We have to do some computation to get abundance
estimates.

00:49:11.650 --> 00:49:13.280
You'll get multiple samples together.

00:49:13.280 --> 00:49:16.990
You'll normalize them to account for sampling
specific artifacts.

00:49:16.990 --> 00:49:21.920
And then we'll get down here to a problem
of differential expression.

00:49:21.920 --> 00:49:27.350
We want to know which genes are significantly
differentially expressed across different

00:49:27.350 --> 00:49:28.350
conditions.

00:49:28.350 --> 00:49:33.369
And the basic data structure that comes into
the calculation-- we've got genes on the rows.

00:49:33.369 --> 00:49:40.339
We got different samples along here, and the
samples can be bundled up into multiple conditions.

00:49:40.339 --> 00:49:44.280
The basic measurement, after all that processing,
is some basic measure of expression abundance

00:49:44.280 --> 00:49:47.850
of a gene i in the sample.

00:49:47.850 --> 00:49:52.109
I'm going to just say briefly something about
a popular technique called EBSeq, which is

00:49:52.109 --> 00:49:57.589
deploying one of these information-sharing
tools in the context of expression.

00:49:57.589 --> 00:50:02.849
And it's a popular one because it has some
nice statistical properties.

00:50:02.849 --> 00:50:05.530
You can get it at those places.

00:50:05.530 --> 00:50:11.079
Let me say quickly, I'm at my 11:50, but I'm
just going to just finish up with a couple

00:50:11.079 --> 00:50:14.450
of slides on the structure of this if that's
OK, Alex.

00:50:14.450 --> 00:50:16.349
Yes, it is.

00:50:16.349 --> 00:50:17.609
Keep going.

00:50:17.609 --> 00:50:18.609
Thanks.

00:50:18.609 --> 00:50:20.589
So how does the structure of this thing go?

00:50:20.589 --> 00:50:23.220
You've got multiple cell conditions.

00:50:23.220 --> 00:50:25.220
You've got gene level parameters.

00:50:25.220 --> 00:50:29.030
So these are parameters that have something
to do with the distribution of the expression

00:50:29.030 --> 00:50:32.230
of measurements in each condition.

00:50:32.230 --> 00:50:36.790
[INAUDIBLE] replicated expression profiles
in various conditions-- one, [INAUDIBLE] two,

00:50:36.790 --> 00:50:37.900
and so on.

00:50:37.900 --> 00:50:40.740
And each gene i is going to have some property.

00:50:40.740 --> 00:50:45.710
So for example, if we took the negative binomial
distribution, which is an overdispersed--

00:50:45.710 --> 00:50:52.930
a natural distribution to use for count data,
I show for two different thetas here possibly

00:50:52.930 --> 00:50:58.119
two different conditions on a given gene,
what the sampling distribution of the counts

00:50:58.119 --> 00:51:01.930
might be like for different for expression
values here.

00:51:01.930 --> 00:51:09.280
If 0.1 was the expression, then if theta of
0.1 was the value, then this would be the

00:51:09.280 --> 00:51:16.230
fluctuation we'd expect to see of expression
measurements in that condition.

00:51:16.230 --> 00:51:18.430
So this is a natural probability distribution
to use.

00:51:18.430 --> 00:51:19.430
Many people use it.

00:51:19.430 --> 00:51:21.950
But how can you use it in a way to borrow
strength?

00:51:21.950 --> 00:51:27.000
At issue are, you want to find genes where
there may be really some difference in the

00:51:27.000 --> 00:51:30.380
thetas between conditions.

00:51:30.380 --> 00:51:35.160
So coming to the borrowing strength part,
we have a bunch of related but separate problems,

00:51:35.160 --> 00:51:37.700
those being all the different genes.

00:51:37.700 --> 00:51:42.100
And we have parameters theta, one for each
gene, and each one is a vector over the different

00:51:42.100 --> 00:51:43.900
conditions.

00:51:43.900 --> 00:51:51.440
And the key structure of EBSeq is to do a
first discrete mixing over so-called patterns

00:51:51.440 --> 00:51:56.369
of agreement and disagreement between the
condition-level distributions.

00:51:56.369 --> 00:52:00.440
And then for any latent variables-- so that's
a finite sum here.

00:52:00.440 --> 00:52:03.350
I'll give a quick example of that subsequently.

00:52:03.350 --> 00:52:05.550
And then you'll still have unknown thetas.

00:52:05.550 --> 00:52:10.560
Even for a given pattern, you'll have unknown
thetas, and they'll come out of some mixing

00:52:10.560 --> 00:52:11.650
distributions.

00:52:11.650 --> 00:52:17.190
EBSeq also has some extra bells and whistles
that handle isofarms nicely, and some other

00:52:17.190 --> 00:52:19.770
things I won't say anything about.

00:52:19.770 --> 00:52:23.849
So that's a specific structural model for
that distribution g.

00:52:23.849 --> 00:52:27.940
And we want to learn that from the data, so
we use the [INAUDIBLE] to do it.

00:52:27.940 --> 00:52:31.079
Once we've learned it, we can apply Bayesian-style
calculation.

00:52:31.079 --> 00:52:34.240
What's the probability of a given pattern
given data?

00:52:34.240 --> 00:52:35.890
And here's where the patterns come in.

00:52:35.890 --> 00:52:39.839
For instance, one pattern-- if you have two
conditions, one pattern is that the thetas

00:52:39.839 --> 00:52:42.550
on that gene for the two conditions are the
same.

00:52:42.550 --> 00:52:45.340
There's just the no-differential expression.

00:52:45.340 --> 00:52:49.410
There's no real difference between the two
conditions and the average expression of the

00:52:49.410 --> 00:52:50.410
gene.

00:52:50.410 --> 00:52:53.590
Or another pattern might be there really is
a difference in expression on the average

00:52:53.590 --> 00:53:00.079
between those two conditions and the expression
of that gene-- basic null hypothesis, alternative

00:53:00.079 --> 00:53:01.869
hypothesis at the gene level.

00:53:01.869 --> 00:53:07.000
But the key thing about EBSeq is it's leveraging
all the information across the genome to try

00:53:07.000 --> 00:53:11.190
and improve sensitivity and improve statistical
properties of the final procedure.

00:53:11.190 --> 00:53:16.420
A part of that are local so-called local FDRs--
probability of the null being true given the

00:53:16.420 --> 00:53:17.450
data.

00:53:17.450 --> 00:53:21.420
And the list of discovery genes would be the
things where that null probability is small.

00:53:21.420 --> 00:53:25.930
And then if the null is small, then there's
a high probability of the alternative.

00:53:25.930 --> 00:53:29.800
So these are posterior probabilities calculated
[INAUDIBLE] the model, and there's a bunch

00:53:29.800 --> 00:53:38.050
of nifty formulas that you can work out for
those probabilities in the case that's considered.

00:53:38.050 --> 00:53:42.330
This just shows in a stem-cell example how
it would deploy, where there's two conditions--

00:53:42.330 --> 00:53:47.500
human embryonic stem cells, induced pluripotent
stem cells, different genes on this axis.

00:53:47.500 --> 00:53:51.860
These genes were discovered by the EBSeq analysis
of 5 percent FDR.

00:53:51.860 --> 00:53:56.570
And there were some nice features about that
about power and robustness that other methods

00:53:56.570 --> 00:53:58.370
don't have such good properties on.

00:53:58.370 --> 00:54:00.770
That's a pretty quick summary of that.

00:54:00.770 --> 00:54:05.740
I want to just go to my final couple of slides
on closing remarks, and I appreciate your

00:54:05.740 --> 00:54:06.740
attention.

00:54:06.740 --> 00:54:13.330
Benefits of Bayesian analysis-- handling uncertainty,
as I tried to describe with the flu problem,

00:54:13.330 --> 00:54:16.180
where you've got a whole bunch of things you
don't know, [INAUDIBLE] marginal posterior

00:54:16.180 --> 00:54:20.690
structure, combining different sorts of information,
of data.

00:54:20.690 --> 00:54:23.839
Bayes is natural for that using the rules
of probability.

00:54:23.839 --> 00:54:26.079
Borrowing
strength when you have many related but separate

00:54:26.079 --> 00:54:30.890
problems, and how you can use the collection
of those to improve their statistical properties.

00:54:30.890 --> 00:54:32.599
There's a whole bunch of issues.

00:54:32.599 --> 00:54:34.580
There's computational challenges.

00:54:34.580 --> 00:54:40.040
Where a lot of statistical machine learning
procedures involve optimization, Bayesian

00:54:40.040 --> 00:54:41.810
procedures use a lot of integration.

00:54:41.810 --> 00:54:45.780
So there are some different issues that come
up, and there's a lot of challenges-- data

00:54:45.780 --> 00:54:46.820
science challenges there.

00:54:46.820 --> 00:54:52.100
A big issue, which I think is something we
all need to think about as we do this, is

00:54:52.100 --> 00:54:57.550
that this is all built on model specifications,
and you can make mistakes when you specify

00:54:57.550 --> 00:54:58.640
a probability model.

00:54:58.640 --> 00:55:00.970
The specification might be too simple.

00:55:00.970 --> 00:55:05.470
If it's too simple, you're not going to learn
much new from some-- compared to some simple

00:55:05.470 --> 00:55:06.470
procedure.

00:55:06.470 --> 00:55:08.589
If it's too complicated, well, you risk all
kinds of things.

00:55:08.589 --> 00:55:11.430
You risk making mistakes in the deployment
of it.

00:55:11.430 --> 00:55:18.990
You risk that it doesn't respect the properties
of the data sufficiently well.

00:55:18.990 --> 00:55:20.700
So there's risks associated.

00:55:20.700 --> 00:55:26.700
And then the question in any given situation
of the risks of making a screwy model specification,

00:55:26.700 --> 00:55:31.440
do they outweigh the benefits of improved
power or improved sensitivity?

00:55:31.440 --> 00:55:38.740
And then finally, this last, I'll say the
unknowns you don't know, you don't know.

00:55:38.740 --> 00:55:43.130
Bayes is all about unknowns and knowns, and
a joint probability distribution over all

00:55:43.130 --> 00:55:47.830
those things-- [INAUDIBLE] priors, the probability
distribution of the thing that you don't know,

00:55:47.830 --> 00:55:51.220
and then the likelihood is the probability
the data that you will get to know conditional

00:55:51.220 --> 00:55:52.220
on what you don't know.

00:55:52.220 --> 00:55:57.240
And you work out a posterior distribution
of what you don't know given what you know.

00:55:57.240 --> 00:56:02.890
Except it doesn't work if-- you can't put
a prior down on something you don't know if

00:56:02.890 --> 00:56:05.310
you don't know that you don't know it.

00:56:05.310 --> 00:56:09.960
And this is a fundamental issue that we have
to deal with.

00:56:09.960 --> 00:56:15.609
The way it's dealt with in practice is essentially
through diagnostic procedures.

00:56:15.609 --> 00:56:21.140
Obviously, all analyses are facing this thing,
but we have to be upfront about it.

00:56:21.140 --> 00:56:24.859
And so any model-based calculations we ever
attempt to do, we need to accompany with predictive

00:56:24.859 --> 00:56:29.480
checks and diagnostics to make sure that we're
not really fooling ourselves.

00:56:29.480 --> 00:56:36.190
I just want to end by dedicating this short
lecture to Steve Fienberg, who passed away

00:56:36.190 --> 00:56:38.940
too soon last year.

00:56:38.940 --> 00:56:45.760
He was a tremendous figure, had done tons
of things for the field of statistics generally

00:56:45.760 --> 00:56:51.200
and data science and Bayesian analysis, and
he had a positive impact on lots of people's

00:56:51.200 --> 00:56:55.440
careers, and I want to thank him for his contributions.

00:56:55.440 --> 00:56:58.579
And I just want to say thanks again to you
all for listening.

00:56:58.579 --> 00:57:02.910
And I know it was quick, but the slides are
available for you to look over.

00:57:02.910 --> 00:57:05.820
And here are some papers that I cited during.

00:57:05.820 --> 00:57:07.900
So thanks very much.

00:57:07.900 --> 00:57:08.940
Thanks, Michael.

00:57:08.940 --> 00:57:14.490
That was a great overview for Bayesian inference,
and you covered a lot of ground in a very

00:57:14.490 --> 00:57:19.070
short amount of time, and I appreciate what
a great job you did expanding a lot of the

00:57:19.070 --> 00:57:23.279
more challenging concepts in this space.

00:57:23.279 --> 00:57:29.030
We have time for one quick question, which
is somewhat linked to the last point that

00:57:29.030 --> 00:57:33.120
you brought up about unknowns.

00:57:33.120 --> 00:57:37.849
And I guess the challenge that we had a lot
of times when we're dealing with data sets

00:57:37.849 --> 00:57:44.119
is one of
both missingness and sparseness.

00:57:44.119 --> 00:57:48.310
So I don't know if there are any new techniques
that you could comment on in terms of the

00:57:48.310 --> 00:57:55.760
Bayesian perspective of how to deal with those
two very separate issues, but if there are

00:57:55.760 --> 00:58:02.190
recommendations or suggestions you could make
in that regard.

00:58:02.190 --> 00:58:07.300
Well, I gather next week, I think, Lance Waller
is going to be talking about missingness,

00:58:07.300 --> 00:58:12.300
so I'm sure he'll have more useful things
to say about the first issue of missingness.

00:58:12.300 --> 00:58:13.300
But you're right.

00:58:13.300 --> 00:58:17.920
I mean, very often where you're trying to
do your statistical analysis is in a kind

00:58:17.920 --> 00:58:22.200
of a gray zone where there's really a lot
of stuff you don't have that you would like

00:58:22.200 --> 00:58:23.890
to have.

00:58:23.890 --> 00:58:26.400
And while that's a challenge, it's also a
bit of an opportunity.

00:58:26.400 --> 00:58:28.600
It's like, what else are you going to do?

00:58:28.600 --> 00:58:34.420
If you-- the Bayes strategy provides a sort
of general approach.

00:58:34.420 --> 00:58:43.730
And then there's a lot of liberty that you
have in terms of how you approach it, and

00:58:43.730 --> 00:58:48.859
what you do depends so much on the context
of the situation, exactly what the relevant

00:58:48.859 --> 00:58:53.750
information that you have or the relevant
information that you don't know.

00:58:53.750 --> 00:59:00.200
How can you usefully summarize the data, and
what parts of data should you not have summarized

00:59:00.200 --> 00:59:03.380
away?

00:59:03.380 --> 00:59:12.109
I think you need to sit yourself in this kind
of a Goldilocks scenario where the model might

00:59:12.109 --> 00:59:15.060
be too simple, the model might be too complicated.

00:59:15.060 --> 00:59:19.540
If the problem that you're addressing is sufficiently
important, you'll have enough time to think

00:59:19.540 --> 00:59:22.380
hard about it, and you could try something
like this.

00:59:22.380 --> 00:59:26.250
And essentially, what you need to do is write
down some kind of probability structure for

00:59:26.250 --> 00:59:28.700
all those things that you don't know.

00:59:28.700 --> 00:59:33.450
And exactly what that would look like, that
depends very much on the context, doesn't

00:59:33.450 --> 00:59:34.450
it?

00:59:34.450 --> 00:59:41.020
I would say that in some classical probability
models, like regression models, where sparsity

00:59:41.020 --> 00:59:45.869
is an issue that's very often discussed with
these penalized estimation procedures, lassos

00:59:45.869 --> 00:59:55.280
and all that, that more fully Bayesian tools
for understanding the good procedures in those

00:59:55.280 --> 01:00:01.599
situations have recently been, I think, discovered
and debated.

01:00:01.599 --> 01:00:05.550
So there is a group at work that's doing a
lot with that.

01:00:05.550 --> 01:00:09.930
There's Nick Polson's group in Chicago that's
done a bunch of interesting things.

01:00:09.930 --> 01:00:14.920
So there's the role and the structure of prior
distributions when there's sparsity.

01:00:14.920 --> 01:00:18.410
So that is an active area.

01:00:18.410 --> 01:00:24.109
Maybe that's as much as I can usefully say
about it at the moment, but yeah, it's a good

01:00:24.109 --> 01:00:25.109
question.

01:00:25.109 --> 01:00:26.109
Great.

01:00:26.109 --> 01:00:32.440
Well, we're at 10 o'clock, so I want to thank
you for your time, and we'll end here.

01:00:32.440 --> 01:00:33.440
OK.

01:00:33.440 --> 01:00:35.030
Thanks, Alex.

01:00:35.030 --> 01:00:36.760
Thank you.

